## Testing the formatter

Each case formats a small loom and reads back the text. A loom written inside this loom's code would be read as part of it, so a fixture is a list of its lines, each one quoted and indented, and `lines` joins them into the text a file holds. `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

The fixtures live at a path no workspace holds, so they format under the defaults: Loom's own anchor delimiters and a width of a hundred. Each declares TypeScript in its frontmatter. A case that needs a TypeScript formatter supplies `squeezeAt`, a stand-in service whose `format` turns every run of spaces inside a line into one space. It squeezes only when it is asked for the width it was made with, so a case that expects the change also checks that the width arrived.

=>

import { describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { Effect, Layer } from 'effect'
import { LoomFormatter } from '../src/LoomFormatter'
import { PackageConfig } from '../src/PackageConfig'
import { defineLanguageService, type LanguageService } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService'

const it = effectify(it_)

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const path = '/nowhere/formatted.loom'

const squeezeAt = (width: number): LanguageService =>
  defineLanguageService({
    id: 'typescript',
    displayName: 'Squeeze',
    extensions: ['.ts'],
    plugins: () => Effect.succeed([]),
    format: (request) =>
      Effect.succeed(
        request.width === width
          ? request.code.replace(/(\S) {2,}(?=\S)/g, '$1 ')
          : request.code,
      ),
  })

const formatted = (text: string, languages: ReadonlyArray<LanguageService> = []) =>
  LoomFormatter.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((formatter) => formatter.format(path, text, languages)),
    Effect.provide(LoomFormatter.layer.pipe(Layer.provide(PackageConfig.layer))),
  )

const frontmatter = ['---', 'Language: TypeScript', '---', '']

## Joining prose

A paragraph written over three lines comes back on one, its words joined by single spaces. The other blocks of Markdown keep their lines: a list item takes in the line that continues it but not the item after it, a fence keeps every line inside it, a heading and a table keep theirs, and a line that ends in two spaces keeps its break.

=>

describe('LoomFormatter — prose', () => {
  it.effect('joins the lines of a paragraph into one', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const text = lines(...frontmatter, '# Notes', '', 'One paragraph', 'written over', '   three lines.', '')
      expect(yield* formatted(text)).toBe(
        lines(...frontmatter, '# Notes', '', 'One paragraph written over three lines.', ''),
      )
    }),
  )

  it.effect('keeps the lines of lists, fences, tables and hard breaks', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const text = lines(
        ...frontmatter,
        '# Notes',
        '',
        '- an item',
        '  that continues',
        '- the next item',
        '',
        '```',
        'kept',
        'as is',
        '```',
        '',
        '| a | b |',
        '| c | d |',
        '',
        'a break  ',
        'stays',
        '',
      )
      expect(yield* formatted(text)).toBe(
        lines(
          ...frontmatter,
          '# Notes',
          '',
          '- an item that continues',
          '- the next item',
          '',
          '```',
          'kept',
          'as is',
          '```',
          '',
          '| a | b |',
          '| c | d |',
          '',
          'a break  ',
          'stays',
          '',
        ),
      )
    }),
  )

  it.effect('changes nothing twice', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const once = yield* formatted(lines(...frontmatter, '# Notes', '', 'Two', 'lines.', ''))
      expect(yield* formatted(once)).toBe(once)
    }),
  )
})

## Formatting code

Without a formatter for its language, a code section keeps its spacing. With a formatter made for the default width of a hundred, the doubled spaces in a section's code close up, the code's own line breaks stay, and the prose around the code is untouched. A change inside a section that an anchor composes lands in that section, where the author wrote the code. A formatter made for another width changes nothing, which shows the width the formatter receives is the project's.

A formatter's semicolon lands where a section's code ends. `semicolons` ends every line of code with one, and the line `Greeting` contributes ends where the root goes on past the anchor, a point that is two places in the loom. The semicolon closes the statement before that point, so it lands at the end of `Greeting`'s own line.

A root takes all of a formatter's real changes or none. `voided` starts the line `Greeting` contributes with `void`, at the same point, but what it inserts opens the next statement rather than closing the last. It has nowhere to land, so the whole root stays as the author wrote it, the spacing changes that would have landed included.

=>

describe('LoomFormatter — code', () => {
  const program = lines(
    ...frontmatter,
    '# Program [program.ts]',
    '',
    'Some prose.',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    'const  answer  =  42',
    anchor('Greeting'),
    '',
    '# Greeting',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    "console.log('hi',  answer)",
  )

  it.effect('leaves code alone without a formatter for its language', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      expect(yield* formatted(program)).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

  it.effect('formats each section through its language, in place', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      expect(yield* formatted(program, [squeezeAt(100)])).toBe(
        lines(
          ...frontmatter,
          '# Program [program.ts]',
          '',
          'Some prose.',
          '',
          '=>',
          '',
          'const answer = 42',
          anchor('Greeting'),
          '',
          '# Greeting',
          '',
          '=>',
          '',
          "console.log('hi', answer)",
        ),
      )
    }),
  )

  const formatterOf = (format: (code: string) => string): LanguageService =>
    defineLanguageService({
      id: 'typescript',
      displayName: 'Stand-in',
      extensions: ['.ts'],
      plugins: () => Effect.succeed([]),
      format: (request) => Effect.succeed(format(request.code)),
    })

  it.effect('lands a closing semicolon at the end of the section before it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const semicolons = formatterOf((code) => code.replace(/(\S)\n/g, '$1;\n'))
      expect(yield* formatted(program, [semicolons])).toBe(
        program
          .replace('const  answer  =  42\n', 'const  answer  =  42;\n')
          .replace("console.log('hi',  answer)\n", "console.log('hi',  answer);\n"),
      )
    }),
  )

  it.effect('leaves a root whose change has nowhere to land', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const voided = formatterOf((code) =>
        code.replace(/(\S) {2,}(?=\S)/g, '$1 ').replace(/^console/m, 'void console'),
      )
      expect(yield* formatted(program, [voided])).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

  it.effect('hands the formatter the project width', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      expect(yield* formatted(program, [squeezeAt(80)])).toBe(program)
    }),
  )
})

